{"id":"8b9beb09-507a-4359-bc0c-8027737eece2","type":"level","createdDate":"2026-08-10T21:30:21.894Z","createdBy":"bill.marsland@codeninjas.com","lastModifiedDate":"2026-08-10T21:30:21.894Z","lastModifiedBy":"bill.marsland@codeninjas.com","attributes":{"tags":["CREATE"],"title":"Level 2","sequence":2,"activity":{"id":"b4f8c36a-d5f6-45a2-a3de-245966922c35","type":"activity","sequence":1,"group":{"id":"b4f8c36a-d5f6-45a2-a3de-245966922c35","type":"build","name":null,"sequence":2,"subgroups":[{"id":"b4f8c36a-d5f6-45a2-a3de-245966922c35","type":"subgroup","sequence":1,"name":"build 1","steps":[{"id":"551357af-b843-4f3b-9e37-0eb04c272633","hints":"<p><span style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\">❓</span><span style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(255, 255, 255);\">Did you drag the </span><strong style=\"background-color: rgb(255, 0, 0); color: rgb(255, 255, 255);\">[set sprite to]</strong><span style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(255, 255, 255);\"> block </span><em style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(255, 255, 255);\">inside</em><span style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(255, 255, 255);\"> the </span><strong style=\"background-color: rgb(0, 255, 0); color: rgb(255, 255, 255);\">[on start]</strong><span style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(255, 255, 255);\"> block?</span></p>","senseiHelp":"<p><strong>Solution:</strong></p><p><img src=\"https://assets.codeninjas.com/clm/assets/images/rtqvaqla.4po.png\"></p><p><strong>Ask the ninja:</strong></p><p>❓Did you explore the Gallery and select an image for the sprite?</p>","instructions":"<p>Add a duck <strong>sprite</strong> to this project! </p><p><br></p><p>Open the<strong> Sprites </strong>code block menu, drag a<strong> [set sprite to] </strong>block inside the<strong> [on start]</strong> block, then click the gray box to select a <strong>sprite </strong>from the Gallery.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>Click ▶ to see the <strong>sprite</strong> appear on the screen!</p>","text":null,"type":null,"image":null,"videoUrl":null,"mediaType":null,"questions":null,"badges":null,"reflectionQuestions":null},{"id":"6499c7b7-55e4-48d4-a1d0-89d9797db855","hints":"<p>❓ Did you click on the ⏷in the red box?</p><p><img src=\"data:image/png;base64,\"></p>","senseiHelp":"<p><strong>Solution:</strong></p><p><strong><img src=\"https://assets.codeninjas.com/clm/assets/images/mzardcrv.re4.png\">﻿</strong></p><p><br></p><p><strong>Ask the ninja:</strong></p><p>❓ What might be a good name for your sprite that is simple to type and understand?</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Sensei Pro tip</strong>:&nbsp;</p><p>💡Encourage the ninja to give <strong>sprites </strong>simple yet descriptive names, as that will be helpful to refer to <strong>sprites </strong>clearly.</p>","instructions":"<p>Rename the <strong>sprite</strong>! </p><p><br></p><p>In the<strong> [set sprite to]</strong> block, click the ⏷ in the <strong>[mySprite] </strong>name box<strong><em> </em></strong>to open the drop down menu<strong><em>, </em></strong>then<strong><em> </em></strong>select <strong><em>Rename variable… </em></strong></p><p><br></p><p><strong><em><span class=\"ql-cursor\">﻿</span></em></strong>Give the <strong>sprite</strong> a simple name that describes what it is, such as “duck”.</p>","text":null,"type":null,"image":null,"videoUrl":null,"mediaType":null,"questions":null,"badges":null,"reflectionQuestions":null},{"id":"1598a933-8aba-4e7b-b8c1-8b39cc791ddb","hints":"<p>❓ Did you select the correct <strong>sprite </strong>name in the <strong>[set sprite position to]</strong> block?</p>","senseiHelp":"<p><strong>Solution:</strong></p><p><img src=\"https://assets.codeninjas.com/clm/assets/images/axyp2ldg.woc.png\"></p><p><br></p><p><strong>Ask the ninja</strong>:&nbsp;</p><p>❓ Did you drag the <strong>[set sprite position to]</strong> block <em>inside</em> the <strong>[on start]</strong> container block, <em>underneath</em> the <strong>[set sprite to]</strong> block?</p>","instructions":"<p>Add a block to move the duck <strong>sprite</strong> to a different position. </p><p><br></p><p>Open the <strong>Sprites </strong>code block menu and drag a<strong> [set sprite position to]</strong> block underneath the<strong> [set sprite to] </strong>block inside the<strong> [on start] </strong>container block. </p><p><br></p><p>Click the ⏷ on the<strong> (mySprite) </strong>bubble<strong><em> </em></strong>to open the drop down menu<strong><em>, </em></strong>then<strong><em> </em></strong>select the duck <strong>sprite</strong>’s name.</p>","text":null,"type":null,"image":null,"videoUrl":null,"mediaType":null,"questions":null,"badges":null,"reflectionQuestions":null},{"id":"2729efde-f8d0-4686-ac8c-69579120e1b5","hints":"<p><span style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(255, 255, 255);\">❓ Did you try both larger and smaller values for the</span><strong style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(255, 255, 255);\"> x</strong><span style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(255, 255, 255);\"> and for the</span><strong style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(255, 255, 255);\"> y</strong><span style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(255, 255, 255);\"> to see how it changes the sprite’s position?&nbsp; </span></p>","senseiHelp":"<p><strong>Solution:</strong></p><p><img src=\"https://assets.codeninjas.com/clm/assets/images/m1ovs3mi.fsc.png\"></p><p><br></p><p><strong>Ask the ninja</strong>:&nbsp;</p><p>❓ Where do you want to position your sprite on the screen?</p><p><br></p>","instructions":"<p>Now, pick the duck <strong>sprite’s</strong> new position! </p><p><br></p><p>Click in the white bubbles to change the <strong>x</strong> and <strong>y</strong> coordinate values. The <strong>x</strong> value will move the sprite left or right, the <strong>y</strong> value will move the sprite up or down.</p><p><br></p><p>Click ▶ to see the new position of the duck <strong>sprite</strong>.</p>","text":null,"type":null,"image":null,"videoUrl":null,"mediaType":null,"questions":null,"badges":null,"reflectionQuestions":null},{"id":"385e60e2-e96c-4c42-a38f-8eed7577f56a","hints":"<p><span style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(255, 255, 255);\">❓ Did you set the correct </span><strong style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(255, 255, 255);\">sprite </strong><span style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(255, 255, 255);\">name in the </span><strong style=\"background-color: rgb(74, 134, 232); color: rgb(255, 255, 255);\">[sprite say]</strong><span style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(255, 255, 255);\"> block?</span></p>","senseiHelp":"<p><strong>Solution:</strong></p><p><img src=\"https://assets.codeninjas.com/clm/assets/images/dlc05avl.gjt.png\"></p><p><br></p><p><strong>Ask the ninja</strong>:&nbsp;</p><p>❓ What do you want your sprite to say?</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Sensei Pro Tip</strong>:&nbsp;</p><p>💡 Guide the ninja to notice that the order of the code blocks matters. Any block that refers to a <strong>sprite </strong>needs to come after its<strong> [set sprite to] </strong>block. </p>","instructions":"<p>Make the duck <strong>sprite</strong> say something! </p><p><br></p><p>Open the <strong>Sprites </strong>code block menu and drag a<strong> [sprite say] </strong>block underneath the other blocks inside the <strong>[on start]</strong> block. </p><p><br></p><p>Click the ⏷ on the<strong> (mySprite) </strong>bubble<strong><em> </em></strong>to open the drop down menu<strong><em>, </em></strong>then<strong><em> </em></strong>select the <strong>sprite</strong>’s name. </p><p><br></p><p>Click in the white bubble and type something for the <strong>sprite</strong> to say.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>Click ▶ to see what the duck <strong>sprite</strong> says on screen.</p>","text":null,"type":null,"image":null,"videoUrl":null,"mediaType":null,"questions":null,"badges":null,"reflectionQuestions":null},{"id":"9e682475-666e-4e88-85f8-394d13269b1d","hints":"<p><span style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(255, 255, 255);\">💡The </span><strong style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(255, 255, 255);\">sequence </strong><span style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(255, 255, 255);\">of the code blocks matters. Putting the blocks in a different order will change how the program runs.</span></p>","senseiHelp":"<p><strong>Solution:</strong></p><p><img src=\"https://assets.codeninjas.com/clm/assets/images/ue3ihisc.r5z.png\"></p><p><br></p><p><strong>Ask the ninja</strong>:&nbsp;</p><p>❓ Did you explore the sound dropdown menu and select one that you like?</p><p>❓ Did you place the<strong> [play sound]</strong> code block at the end, after all the duck sprite blocks?</p><p><br></p><p><strong style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\">Sensei Pro Tip</strong><span style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\">:&nbsp;</span></p><p><span style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\">💡 The </span><strong style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\">[play sound]</strong><span style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\"> block’s playback mode parameter should be left as </span><strong style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\">[until done]</strong><span style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\"> so the code that follows this block does not run until the sound has finished playing. Ninjas will explore other playback mode options in a later level.</span></p>","instructions":"<p>Make the duck <strong>sprite</strong> play a sound!</p><p><br></p><p>Open the <strong>Music </strong>code block menu and drag a <strong>[play sound]</strong> block underneath the other blocks inside the <strong>[on start]</strong> block. Click the ⏷ to open the drop down menu, then select a new sound.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>Allow the sound to play <strong>[until done]</strong>. This will pause the code from running, until the sound has finished playing.</p><p><br></p><p>Click ▶ to hear the sound the duck&nbsp;<strong>sprite</strong> makes! </p>","text":null,"type":null,"image":null,"videoUrl":null,"mediaType":null,"questions":null,"badges":null,"reflectionQuestions":null},{"id":"72b33e6c-8c8b-4550-a908-599e977f90f0","hints":"<p><span style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(255, 255, 255);\">❓ Did you put your </span><strong style=\"background-color: rgb(183, 183, 183); color: rgb(255, 255, 255);\">[set background color to]</strong><span style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(255, 255, 255);\"> block </span><em style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(255, 255, 255);\">above</em><span style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(255, 255, 255);\"> the other blocks in your code?</span></p>","senseiHelp":"<p><strong>Solution:</strong></p><p><img src=\"https://assets.codeninjas.com/clm/assets/images/y4gt0p13.ksa.png\"></p><p><br></p><p><strong>Ask the ninja</strong>:&nbsp;</p><p>❓ Why do you think the <strong>[set background color to]</strong> block should go at the very top of the code? What would happen if you put it at the very bottom?</p>","instructions":"<p>Add a background color! </p><p><br></p><p>Open the<strong> Scene </strong>code block menu and drag a <strong>[set background color to]</strong> block above the other blocks inside the <strong>[on start]</strong> block. Click on the bubble to select a color.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>Click ▶ to see the screen color change!</p>","text":null,"type":null,"image":null,"videoUrl":null,"mediaType":null,"questions":null,"badges":null,"reflectionQuestions":null},{"id":"b4b0075b-064b-4399-8116-05243b69283d","hints":"<p><span style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(255, 255, 255);\">❓ Did you set the correct </span><strong style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(255, 255, 255);\">sprite </strong><span style=\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(255, 255, 255);\">name in the new code blocks?</span></p>","senseiHelp":"<p><strong>Solution:</strong></p><p><img src=\"https://assets.codeninjas.com/clm/assets/images/kxsofgk5.vrc.png\"></p><p><br></p><p><strong>Ask the ninja</strong>:</p><p>❓Which blocks did you use for your first <strong>sprite</strong>?</p><p>❓Which block will set your <strong>sprite </strong>image?<em> </em>Move your <strong>sprite’s </strong>position? Play a sound?</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Sensei Pro tip</strong>:&nbsp;</p><p>💡Encourage the ninja to look back at how they programmed their first <strong>sprite</strong> for help in programming their new sprite.</p>","instructions":"<p>Add another <strong>sprite</strong> to finish this project! </p><p><br></p><p>Use a <strong>[set sprite to] </strong>block to create another <strong>sprite</strong>, then click the ⏷ in the <strong>[mySprite] </strong>name box<strong><em> </em></strong>to open the drop down menu then select <strong><em>Rename variable…</em></strong> to give the <strong>sprite</strong> a new name.</p><p><br></p><p>Now, set the <strong>sprite</strong> position and make the <strong>sprite</strong> say something. Click ⏷on the<strong> (mySprite) </strong>bubble on the code blocks that program the new <strong>sprite </strong>to select the new <strong>sprite’s </strong>name. </p><p><br></p><p>Lastly, add another sound.</p><p><br></p><p>Click ▶ to see both <strong>sprites</strong> on screen!</p>","text":null,"type":null,"image":null,"videoUrl":null,"mediaType":null,"questions":null,"badges":null,"reflectionQuestions":null}],"title":"Meet New Friends! ","project":{"editor":{"filters":[{"name":"sprites","active":false,"blocks":[{"name":"spritescreate","active":true},{"name":"spritesetvel","active":true},{"name":"spritesetpos","active":true},{"name":"Sprite_blockCombine_set","active":true},{"name":"Sprite_blockCombine_get","active":true},{"name":"spriteFollowOtherSprite","active":true},{"name":"startEffectOnSprite","active":true},{"name":"spritedestroy2